The unpredictability of power grid fluctuations stems primarily from the unpredictable nature of energy consumption and unpredictable failures within the grid. Research suggests that the statistical properties of these fluctuations do not conform to a standard Gaussian distribution as one would expect, given the assumption of independent random events. Instead, recent studies indicate that the power grid fluctuations exhibit non-Gaussian properties, which may be attributed to the presence of correlated random processes. In this study, we investigate the power grid fluctuations in the Netherlands and discover that they closely align with a specific type of Levy distribution, which is characterized by heavy-tailed properties. Our findings are consistent with the existing literature and provide strong evidence that the non-Gaussian nature of power grid fluctuations is a widespread phenomenon. Furthermore, our results have significant implications for the development of early warning systems for power grid failures, as they imply that the power grid is more susceptible to rare and extreme events. As a result, identifying these events through early warning systems becomes a critical priority, and our Levy distribution-based approach may be a valuable tool in achieving this goal. Ultimately, this research has the potential to improve the reliability of power grids and reduce the likelihood of grid failures. The data used in this study are publicly available, and readers can access them at the specified URL. The code used to generate the figures and produce the results is also publicly available, as per the study's open-access policy. The data and code can be found at the specified URL. \end{document} \noindent\textbf{Supplementary Materials} \\begin{tabular}{l} \end{tabular}
