This paper describes how to integrate the Fuel compiler with the KVM, 
leveraging the KVM's capabilities to compile a large subset of the 
Scheme language into native machine code.  The compiler, available as open-source, 
utilizes static analysis to ensure memory safety and prevent common errors.
The system can execute Scheme functions in a standalone environment or as a library. 
We also demonstrate a proof-of-concept for a high-performance VM that can run 
single instruction programs compiled by Fuel.  The implementation of this 
project is extensive, with the compiler itself comprising over 20,000 lines 
of SML code and over 30,000 lines of C++ code.  The paper provides a comprehensive 
overview of the system's components, its implementation, and evaluation. 
The system's code is publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/fuelVM/fuel. 
In addition, a more detailed description of the system's design can be found 
in a companion paper at https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02293. 
The implementation of this project was made possible through a grant from NSF 
award 2022273. 
